\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSCustomImageRep is an object that uses a delegated method to render an image. When called upon to produce the image, it sends a message to its delegate to have the method performed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Like most other kinds of NSImageReps, an NSCustomImageRep is generally used indirectly, through an NSImage object. An NSImage must be able to choose between various representations of a given image. It also needs to provide an off-screen cache of the appropriate depth for any image it uses. It determines this information by querying its NSImageReps.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Thus to work with an NSImage, an NSCustomImageRep must be able to provide some information about its image. Use the following methods, inherited from the NSImageRep class, to set these attributes of the NSCustomImageRep:\
\fs14\
\pard \s60 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \fs28 setSize:\
\fs14\
\fs28 setColorSpaceName:\
\fs14\
\fs28 setAlpha:\
\fs14\
\fs28 setPixelsHigh:\
\fs14\
\fs28 setPixelsWide:\
\fs14\
\fs28 setBitsPerSample:\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Note that if these attributes aren\'27t set, and an NSCustomImageRep is used in an NSImage with other representations, NSImage won\'27t be able to select between them. In actual practice, this usually isn\'27t a problem.\
\fs14\
